\title{Memoization and analysis}
\date{2026-10-28T13:44:32Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{Notice that #{\OPT} naturally forms a #{1 \times (n+1)} table where each entry requires #{\Theta(1)} operations to fill in. We can use a recursive function that fills in the table as it goes, or we can just fill in the table using a loop from 0 up to #{n}. Either way, the whole algorithm is obviously #{\Theta(n)}. This is one nice benefit of dynamic programming—analyzing their runtime is usually dead easy!}

